<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Habsburg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Austria, Habsburgs, Habsburg-Lorraine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>, also known as the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, was a European dynasty named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-castle">Habsburg Castle</a>, a Swabian fortress built in the 1020s in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. In 1273 Rudolph was elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and, after his victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a> in 1278, moved the family's power base to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, where it ruled until 1918. The Habsburgs held the throne of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> from 1440 to 1740, and as Habsburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraines</a> from 1765 to 1806. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> abdicated in 1556 the family split into Spanish and Austrian branches, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and much else.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The progenitor of the house may have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntram">Guntram</a> the Rich, a count in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breisgau">Breisgau</a> in the 10th century, though the source was written some 200 years later and much about the early family is uncertain. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radbot-of-klettgau">Radbot of Klettgau</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-castle">Habsburg Castle</a>, and Radbot's grandson Otto II was the first to take the fortress name, as Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>. In the second half of the 13th century Rudolph I was an influential lord between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>. In 1278 he defeated and killed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a> at the Battle of Marchfeld, and by the Georgenberg Pact of 1286 he secured the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> for his family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1379 the brothers Albert III and Leopold III divided the family lands by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-neuberg">Treaty of Neuberg</a> into an Albertinian line, which kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> proper, and a Leopoldian line. Rudolf IV had used the forged privilegium maius to introduce the title of Archduke; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> and his successors refused to recognise it. The lines were reunited in 1490, when Archduke Sigismund of Further Austria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> gave way to Maximilian I.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> abdicated in 1556 the house divided into an Austrian branch under his brother Ferdinand and a Spanish branch under his son Philip II. The Spanish line ruled Portugal from 1580 to 1640. On 11 August 1804 Francis I took the title of Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and on 6 August 1806 he dissolved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. Maximilian, a Habsburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> archduke, became emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and was shot by republican forces in 1867.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> relied on marriages between close relatives, with harmful effects on the family's gene pool: epilepsy, insanity and early death. A study of 3,000 members over 16 generations suggests inbreeding may have played a part in the family's extinction, and a study of 2019 found a significant correlation with the degree of mandibular prognathism, the so-called Habsburg jaw. Other studies dispute a link between fertility and close kinship. Charles I was expelled from his domains after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and the Imperial Crypt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> is the burial place of 150 members of the family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Habsburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With two rival kings elected in Hungary, the Croatian Sabor unanimously elected Ferdinand of the <b>House of Habsburg</b> as King of Croatia at Cetin on 1 January 1527, while the Slavonian nobility sided with John Zápolya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-croatia-1102-1526">Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
